urse connery famously loathed  portraying the renegade secret agent and  eventually dropped out of the series the  next iconic bond would be roger moore  who took up the mantle in  1973. in doing so he restored a sense of  social elegance and sophistication to  the character while also pushing the  series in a notably more lighthearted  direction moore's bond didn't have  connery's smoldering sexuality or bad  boy appeal and while his films leaned  into the character's womanizing even  more heavily than before his politeness  and propriety smoothed off the rougher  edges of bond's machismo he generally  seemed more like an amateur sommelier  than mr steel your girl and on the  surface that might seem like a return to  fleming's bond blueprint this was a bond  who was comfortable in the high class  surroundings which connery had made a  mockery of he exemplified the kind of  mannered intelligence and strategic  brilliance that the british empire  wanted to retain as its personal brand  the only problem was that morris bond  was also the campiest he corralled with  ladies named holly goodhead and got  attacked by ice hockey players  altogether these were ludicrous  semi-periodic films rather than serious  spy thrillers but more was very much in  on the joke portraying bond as a larger  than life lightly humorous figure a  pleasant evening and that may not have  been exactly what fleming had in mind  once again though it was the bond the  audience wanted he was more of an  uncomplicated good guy who the whole  family could cheer for everything else  about his adventures reflected this  desire for light entertainment too the  villains became even more over the top  and the gadgets got notably goofier can  anyone get me like a shoe that has a  knife inside of it does anyone have a  pair of flip-flops that become handcuffs  can someone get me a hat that has a  special gadget where your parents tell  you they love you and your ears this was  all reflective of the general  