ra a post-cold war world in which  the old hierarchies were being  challenged at least to some degree it  wasn't just the way he looked or spoke  but the whole way this new bond had to  interact with the world around him the  hetero male fantasy which the character  represented would no longer go totally  unscrutinized good  because i think you're a sexist  misogynist dinosaur a relic of the cold  war and the movie's female characters  were no longer just there to roll around  in bed with bond they were kicking ass  right alongside him or growing immune to  his charms out on some kind of  professional assignment addressing the  kill  you know you'll find this crushing 07.  when i don't sit at home every night  praying for some international incident  so i can run down here all dressed up to  impress james bond the brosnan bond  movies were an attempt to find a place  for the character in an era of evolving  gender norms that mainstream  entertainment was expected to at least  pay lip service to they even use the  question is there still a place in the  modern world for a secret agent like 007  in promotional materials explicitly  playing to the idea of bond as an  anachronism rather than hiding from it  and while the boston bond movies were  still too light-hearted to fully  persuade us of bond's contemporary  relevance the man who would inherit his  double o status would be far better  equipped in a sense the broodier  bloodier direction which daniel craig  took the bond franchise was really the  only place for it to go by 2006. after  years of full-blown parodies like austin  powers and johnny english took aim at  all the series tropes and cliches the  goofy self-deprecating humor of boston  and more just wouldn't really work  anymore what are you feeding him  why don't you just kill him  no scott i have an even better idea i'm  going to place him in an easily  escapable situation involving an overly  elaborate and exotic death so you  couldn't really expect an au